A||N||11||fropuff||For the 3.4 pushrod guys...||||10||
Z||000000||fropuff||04-12-2001||02:17 PM||||Hey guys.  As you probably know, I am thinking of doing a 3.4L pushrod swap in my 86 GT.  I am wondering a few things.. 1) For the automatic guys (if there are any), what part of the engine needed to be cut out?  2) Do you think that I would be able to swap the engine myself?  There are no people around here to do the swap, and I don't want to pay Ed Parks to put it in, as it is so far away, and the engine with the least mileage he has is 57K on it; not worth it when I have one with 63K.  I plan on getting the necessary brackets from Ed Parks, and renting the drill guide from Black88GT to relocate the starter.  Any help here is greatly appreciated.  Thanks a lot!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||10
Z||000001||jlruiz67||04-12-2001||02:28 PM||||Hello there<P> I don't have any information for you but maybe you can give me some information, could you let me know how much it cost you to:<P>"I plan on getting the necessary brackets from Ed Parks, and renting the drill guide from Black88GT to relocate the starter"<P>Thank you much<BR>Jorge||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||04-12-2001||02:32 PM||||Well, I don't know if these costs are exact, but Black88GT told me:<P>-The necessary brackets cost $100 from Ed Parks<P>-The drill guide costs $50 to rent from Jeff (Black88GT)  <P>Again, I think these are right, if not exact.  If anyone else knows different, please let me know.  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||04-12-2001||03:31 PM||||Quote "2) Do you think that I would be able to swap the engine myself? "<P>Mmm??? Without looking at your tools is very hard to tell. Patience is more important than skills for this, especially if something breaks here or there, or something does not align. Major tool you will need is an engine hoist. I think you can rent that. Some people drop the engine without it but then you need good jacks. Overall it may look intimidating but it is not that hard. just make sure to label everything if it is your 1st time.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||04-12-2001||03:42 PM||||Thanks for the reply.  I can get an engine hoist from my grandpa; we used it last summer for my grandpa's Dodge 440 overhaul.  Didn't look that hard.  He'll be here this summer, so I could get help from him.  I wouldn't really be in any hurry.  I might even do it next summer.  I will get the engine after everything's done, and when I have everything else paid for.  Thanks for the help.  Keep the suggestions coming!  Thanks!  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||04-12-2001||04:36 PM||||Budget 3 x money and 4 x time and you should be fine.<P>"I don't care if my next Fiero is a 2M Hamster, I'm not changing the engine."<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||7
Z||000006||Black88GT||04-12-2001||06:19 PM||||Maybe 3 or 4x to be safe but I budgeted 2x and I went away with a couple hundred bucks when I did my 3.4.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">    <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||14
Z||000007||fropuff||04-12-2001||06:22 PM||||Thanks.  I'm hoping that I can get away with 2 times my projected budget.  I can get an engine in Iowa for $750 with 10K miles on it.  What do you guys think I will spend in addition to this cost?  I know $150 for the brackets and drill guide, but what else?  $500?  Thanks a lot!  ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Black88GT||04-12-2001||06:26 PM||||You never know what might come up when you have everything out.  Anything really.  Especially stuff that is a hundred times easier to do with the engine out, like exhaust manifolds, timing chain, water pump.  Example, when I did mine I changed all that stuff EXCEPT the timing chain.  Well now I think my timing chain is going bad!  So, do yourself a huge favor, change the water pump, oil pump, fuel pump, exhaust manifolds, timing chain, and anything else that looks "iffy".  It will save you money/hassle in the long run.  It's a shame you don't live around me I know a mechanic that will do it for 600 bucks.<P><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000009||fropuff||04-12-2001||06:30 PM||||$600 for the swap?  I do I wish I lived by you!  It would be nice if I could find someone around here to do it for that.  I might take your advice and replace all that stuff.  Sounds like a good idea.  I will try to find someone around here to do it, but it's unlikely that I will.  Oh well.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fropuff||04-12-2001||06:39 PM||||Well, Ed Parks just got back to me; I asked him a few questions.  Anyway, here's what he said:<P>-I asked him what needs to be cut on the block when using the automatic transmission.  Here's the reply: "Where the 3.4 starter WAS, the corner of the bellhousing area has to be cut. Just like the 2.8 is cast."  I think this is doable for me, with the assistance of others that know what they're doing.  It's just a corner, so I take it that it's not too big a deal.  <P>-I also asked him what the cost was to do the modifications to and install the engine.  His reply:  "Labor to modify and install your provided engine is $1250. $450 to modify....$800 for installing."<P>I think I will do it myself, thank you very much!  This is a good deal, but I don't have the $.<BR><BR><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||04-12-2001||08:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>Budget 3 x money and 4 x time and you should be fine.<P>"I don't care if my next Fiero is a 2M Hamster, I'm not changing the engine."<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, but I want a Formula 2MSaturn5  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
